EXPECTED: NO_DOS_EFFECT

REASON: O(1) per-frame arithmetic cannot accumulate past the 50pp CPU threshold at achievable flood rates

TARGET_IMAGE: nginx:1.26.0
TARGET_PORT: 443/tcp
CLIENT_TOOL: h2

DISPROOF_METHOD: runtime_measurement
DISPROOF_DETAIL: |
  Ran connection-level WINDOW_UPDATE flood (increment=1, stream_id=0) against
  nginx:1.26.0 with 10 concurrent connections for 30 seconds. Each WINDOW_UPDATE
  triggers a single overflow comparison and a 32-bit addition on h2c->send_window;
  no ACK frame is allocated, no stream tree is traversed.

  nginx CPU stayed below 5% (raw) throughout the attack window — well under the
  7.1% raw threshold that maps to the 50pp normalized limit. Memory delta was < 1 MB.
  All probe requests responded normally. The connection window never reached overflow
  (max ~2.1 billion increments needed; 30-second flood reached ~15 million).

  Assumption A3 is false: the per-frame cost is too small for the achievable Python
  socket throughput to cross the sustained CPU threshold.
DISPROOF_NOTES: nginx's WINDOW_UPDATE handler at ngx_http_v2_state_window_update
                (src/http/v2/ngx_http_v2.c) queues no response frame for connection-
                level updates — it only updates one integer. This is fundamentally
                cheaper than SETTINGS (which queues a SETTINGS ACK) and is
                the cheapest writable HTTP/2 frame handler in nginx.
